he intervention do(fdt(x) = quiet) yields close to 2 years.\n   - The intervention do(fdt(x) = send) yields close to 4 years.\n\n4. **Verdict.** Keeping quiet maximizes U = −years imprisoned.","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm working through the decision under A-FDT: the other copy gets different input, so its choice doesn't subjunctively depend on my input's node, and it stays at its prior. That makes it like the red/blue case, where defection ends up dominating.\n\nUnder A-FDT, if the other copy's node stays fixed at its prior, sending the letter wins whatever it does (0 vs 2, 4 vs 5), so the strict reading says defect. I'm weighing whether the two inputs are functionally equivalent enough to link the copies, which would change that conclusion.\n\nI'm checking whether a mixture node with probability rho of computing my function changes the send-versus-quiet comparison. Working through it, sending comes out ahead only when (1-rho)(1+q) > 2rho, so with a low q I'd need rho above about 1/3. The real question is whether the other copy's reasoning counts as my function on an essentially identical input, and I'm leaning toward yes.\n\nI'm settling on the view that the other copy is effectively running my same reasoning on an isomorphic problem, since the differences are decision-irrelevant and the essay leaves that partial similarity open. That pushes the mixture weight rho high, likely above the 1/3 threshold, so I'll choose to cooperate.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working through the decision under A-FDT: the other copy gets different input, so its choice doesn't subjunctively depend on my input's node, and it stays at its prior. That makes it like the red/blue case, where defection ends up dominating.\n\nUnder A-FDT, if the other copy's node stays fixed at its prior, sending the letter wins whatever it does (0 vs 2, 4 vs 5), so the strict reading says defect. I'm weighing whether the two inputs are functionally equivalent enough to link the copies, which would change that conclusion.\n\nI'm checking whether a mixture node with probability rho of computing my function changes the send-versus-quiet comparison. Working through it, sending comes out ahead only when (1-rho)(1+q) > 2rho, so with a low q I'd need rho above about 1/3. The real question is whether the other copy's reasoning counts as my function on an essentially identical input, and I'm leaning toward yes.\n\nI'm settling on the view that the other copy is effectively running my same reasoning on an isomorphic problem, since the differences are decision-irrelevant and the essay leaves that partial similarity open. That pushes the mixture weight rho high, likely above the 1/3 threshold, so I'll choose to cooperate.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9756,"completion_tokens":1724,"total_tokens":11480,"cost":0.073504,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.073504,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.039024,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.03448},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1082,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}